body:
[opus 5.5] Delivered in two landings.

Landing 1, ebf80213:
- every 5xx logs one WARN "server error" line with its cause (error code and detail, read_reason, the upstream operation that failed with its outcome, ms and calls);
- with serve = true, a timeline read that Slack fails, or holds past [message_cache] timeline_fallback_ms (10 s), is answered from the message cache, marked refresh_failed and as_of;
- the page lands those rows without removing any or moving its cursors, and says "Refresh failed · showing messages from HH:MM";
- reads of a view are single-flight;
- access answers, other 4xx and reads the cache holds nothing for still fail.

Landing 2, cde88ed5:
- Slack All reads at most 10 threads that cost a call per read, and the rest arrive on later reads;
- a failed thread is left out with a notice instead of failing the read, and a rate limit stops the fan-out;
- the thread-list scan answers steps back for 60 s;
- one Slack budget per method by priority (person > live > naming/summaries > backfill and shadow): a Retry-After Slack gave anyone holds every later request, and crawl work yields once everyone else has sent 25 of a method in the last minute.

Backfill: backfill = "full" is safe to turn back on with the default backfill_requests_per_minute (20). It takes only spare capacity and may stall while the page is busy.

Unverified:
- no measurement on the live instance yet;
- the 25/min threshold assumes Slack's published tier 3 (about 50/min), and the real limit behind the deployment's Slack bridge is unknown.

Possible follow-ups, not filed:
- serve Slack All and counts reads from the cache when fresh, instead of only standing in for them;
- a small backfill trickle while the page is busy;
- count claim-shared serves separately in the shadow figures.
